1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:02,122
You wanted to be a Viking raider.

2
00:00:02,122 --> 00:00:02,897
A fast ship,

3
00:00:02,897 --> 00:00:05,651
a chest of silver, your name in a song.

4
00:00:05,651 --> 00:00:08,379
Instead, it is the middle of the night,

5
00:00:08,379 --> 00:00:10,166
the rain is coming in sideways,

6
00:00:10,166 --> 00:00:12,232
and you are lying on a row of oars

7
00:00:12,232 --> 00:00:14,929
with another man's feet against your ear.

8
00:00:14,929 --> 00:00:16,150
Nobody is singing.

9
00:00:16,150 --> 00:00:17,422
Somebody is bailing.

10
00:00:17,422 --> 00:00:18,513
In a few minutes,

11
00:00:18,513 --> 00:00:20,187
that somebody will be you.

12
00:00:20,187 --> 00:00:21,962
Imagine you are Eirik again,

13
00:00:21,962 --> 00:00:24,671
the Norse farmer from our last chapter,

14
00:00:24,671 --> 00:00:27,307
a few years older and not noticeably richer.

15
00:00:27,307 --> 00:00:29,311
He is still an imagined person,

16
00:00:29,311 --> 00:00:31,902
built from what archaeology and written

17
00:00:31,902 --> 00:00:34,979
sources tell us, not a real man from history.

18
00:00:35,578 --> 00:00:37,117
Last time, your crew backed away

19
00:00:37,117 --> 00:00:39,235
from a fight and went trading instead.

20
00:00:39,690 --> 00:00:42,067
This summer, Bjorn has bigger plans.

21
00:00:42,067 --> 00:00:44,078
He is gathering men for a raid

22
00:00:44,078 --> 00:00:46,896
across the sea, and he wants you on an oar.

23
00:00:47,399 --> 00:00:49,013
Why would a farmer say yes?

24
00:00:49,013 --> 00:00:50,638
Your roof still leaks.

25
00:00:50,997 --> 00:00:52,059
Your neighbor still

26
00:00:52,059 --> 00:00:53,547
argues about that boundary.

27
00:00:53,547 --> 00:00:55,006
And a few pieces of silver

28
00:00:55,006 --> 00:00:56,679
could solve problems that hard

29
00:00:56,679 --> 00:00:59,014
work keeps politely postponing.

30
00:00:59,014 --> 00:01:02,032
The National Museum of Denmark puts it plainly:

31
00:01:02,032 --> 00:01:03,432
expeditions required

32
00:01:03,432 --> 00:01:05,818
great investment in ships and men,

33
00:01:05,818 --> 00:01:08,684
but they could be a profitable business.

34
00:01:08,684 --> 00:01:10,234
Notice the word could.

35
00:01:10,608 --> 00:01:13,191
The only guaranteed part is the rowing.

36
00:01:13,191 --> 00:01:14,222
Early raids,

37
00:01:14,222 --> 00:01:17,027
the museum adds, were quick surprise attacks,

38
00:01:17,027 --> 00:01:19,641
usually involving only a few ships.

39
00:01:19,641 --> 00:01:20,608
In the eight hundreds,

40
00:01:20,608 --> 00:01:23,713
whole armies began crossing the sea as well.

41
00:01:23,713 --> 00:01:25,948
Bjorn's plan is the small kind:

42
00:01:25,948 --> 00:01:27,856
one ship, one crew,

43
00:01:27,856 --> 00:01:31,749
one stretch of coast, and a great deal of optimism.

44
00:01:31,749 --> 00:01:33,840
Bjorn's standing matters here.

45
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:36,923
Leaders kept followers through generosity,

46
00:01:36,923 --> 00:01:38,939
and the museum notes that skaldic

47
00:01:38,939 --> 00:01:41,810
poems praise kings and magnates who gave

48
00:01:41,810 --> 00:01:43,971
gold rings to those who fought for them.

49
00:01:43,971 --> 00:01:45,350
Bjorn is no king,

50
00:01:45,350 --> 00:01:47,376
but feeding his crew is how he keeps one.

51
00:01:47,815 --> 00:01:50,180
Generosity also creates obligations.

52
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:52,247
Once you accept his food,

53
00:01:52,247 --> 00:01:53,823
his ship and his protection,

54
00:01:53,823 --> 00:01:56,315
your word becomes part of the bargain.

55
00:01:56,315 --> 00:01:57,149
Backing out later

56
00:01:57,149 --> 00:01:58,793
would cost more than silver.

57
00:01:59,168 --> 00:02:00,312
It would cost you your

58
00:02:00,312 --> 00:02:01,505
standing in the valley.

59
00:02:01,505 --> 00:02:04,084
What did a crew agreement actually say?

60
00:02:04,084 --> 00:02:06,109
Here the evidence goes quiet.

61
00:02:06,109 --> 00:02:08,781
The Viking Ship Museum in Roskilde

62
00:02:08,781 --> 00:02:10,793
admits it directly: we don't know much

63
00:02:10,793 --> 00:02:13,229
about the crews of Viking Age warships.

64
00:02:13,229 --> 00:02:15,530
Norwegian law, written down later,

65
00:02:15,530 --> 00:02:17,573
in the eleven hundreds and twelve hundreds,

66
00:02:17,573 --> 00:02:20,491
describes ordinary crewmen as young men

67
00:02:20,491 --> 00:02:22,434
chosen by the steersman,

68
00:02:22,434 --> 00:02:25,673
who had no choice about sailing and kept fixed watches.

69
00:02:26,127 --> 00:02:28,268
That law was written for a king's levy,

70
00:02:28,268 --> 00:02:29,900
not a private raid.

71
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:30,331
Still,

72
00:02:30,331 --> 00:02:33,524
no choice about sailing sounds oddly familiar.

73
00:02:33,850 --> 00:02:36,295
So you bring your spear and your round shield,

74
00:02:36,295 --> 00:02:38,437
the same ones from your last voyage.

75
00:02:38,437 --> 00:02:40,686
You still do not own a sword.

76
00:02:41,044 --> 00:02:41,794
Fortunately,

77
00:02:41,794 --> 00:02:44,460
the sea has never once asked to see one.

78
00:02:44,899 --> 00:02:46,946
Aslaug does not give a speech.

79
00:02:46,946 --> 00:02:48,850
She checks your cloak,

80
00:02:48,850 --> 00:02:51,217
your spare shoes and your food bag,

81
00:02:51,217 --> 00:02:55,112
then reminds you that the harvest will not wait for heroes.

82
00:02:55,112 --> 00:02:57,343
You promise to be back in time.

83
00:02:57,343 --> 00:02:59,767
You both know the weather gets a vote.

84
00:03:00,254 --> 00:03:02,964
The ship is bigger than the boat you knew.

85
00:03:02,964 --> 00:03:03,658
For scale,

86
00:03:03,658 --> 00:03:07,632
the Gokstad ship, buried in Norway around the year nine hundred,

87
00:03:07,632 --> 00:03:09,237
had sixteen pairs of oars.

88
00:03:09,237 --> 00:03:11,598
More oars means more rowers,

89
00:03:11,598 --> 00:03:14,780
and more rowers means more people sharing everything,

90
00:03:14,780 --> 00:03:16,163
including the smell.

91
00:03:16,697 --> 00:03:18,113
Oars are for calm water,

92
00:03:18,113 --> 00:03:20,112
narrow channels and landings.

93
00:03:20,567 --> 00:03:21,560
On the open sea,

94
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:23,613
the sail does the heavy work.

95
00:03:23,939 --> 00:03:25,240
Researchers who sail

96
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:28,496
reconstructions today find the wind far more

97
00:03:28,496 --> 00:03:30,885
effective than any number of tired arms.

98
00:03:30,885 --> 00:03:33,176
Your arms are delighted to hear this.

99
00:03:33,176 --> 00:03:34,382
They are still sore.

100
00:03:34,915 --> 00:03:36,140
There is no cabin,

101
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:38,374
no galley and no dry corner.

102
00:03:38,860 --> 00:03:41,271
The ship is open to sky and spray.

103
00:03:41,271 --> 00:03:43,654
Everything you own is either wet,

104
00:03:43,654 --> 00:03:47,054
about to be wet, or underneath someone else.

105
00:03:47,054 --> 00:03:48,542
Sleep comes in shifts.

106
00:03:48,542 --> 00:03:51,306
When the Viking Ship Museum sailed

107
00:03:51,306 --> 00:03:53,303
its reconstruction of a great longship,

108
00:03:53,303 --> 00:03:55,750
it could only provide proper sleeping

109
00:03:55,750 --> 00:03:58,082
places for about half the crew at a time.

110
00:03:58,082 --> 00:04:00,424
The others stayed at their posts,

111
00:04:00,424 --> 00:04:01,794
ready to handle the sail.

112
00:04:01,794 --> 00:04:04,050
The sleepers laid the oars across

113
00:04:04,050 --> 00:04:05,920
the benches to make a kind of floor.

114
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:07,419
Crew logs from those modern

115
00:04:07,419 --> 00:04:09,274
voyages describe nights so cold

116
00:04:09,274 --> 00:04:11,117
that many preferred to stay on

117
00:04:11,117 --> 00:04:13,311
their feet rather than try to sleep.

118
00:04:13,311 --> 00:04:15,085
You discover the same

119
00:04:15,085 --> 00:04:16,839
thing without needing a logbook.

120
00:04:16,839 --> 00:04:17,636
Food is simple,

121
00:04:17,636 --> 00:04:20,020
because it has to survive the journey.

122
00:04:20,020 --> 00:04:23,074
The Viking Ship Museum lists porridge,

123
00:04:23,074 --> 00:04:24,418
flour, dried fish,

124
00:04:24,418 --> 00:04:28,923
butter and bread, with water carried in barrels or vats.

125
00:04:28,923 --> 00:04:30,442
Beer, it notes,

126
00:04:30,442 --> 00:04:33,123
belonged on chieftains' and kings' ships.

127
00:04:33,123 --> 00:04:36,387
Bjorn is generous, but not that generous.

128
00:04:36,387 --> 00:04:38,995
Dried fish travels remarkably well.

129
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:41,612
Researchers from the universities of

130
00:04:41,612 --> 00:04:44,462
Cambridge and Oslo traced Viking Age cod

131
00:04:44,462 --> 00:04:46,586
bones from the trading town of Haithabu

132
00:04:46,586 --> 00:04:49,224
back to Arctic waters off northern Norway.

133
00:04:49,224 --> 00:04:50,603
They could not tell whether

134
00:04:50,603 --> 00:04:52,055
the fish was trade goods or

135
00:04:52,055 --> 00:04:53,964
someone's food for the voyage.

136
00:04:54,465 --> 00:04:55,234
Either way,

137
00:04:55,234 --> 00:04:57,543
it was extremely well-travelled cod.

138
00:04:57,543 --> 00:04:59,324
Hot meals are harder.

139
00:04:59,324 --> 00:05:00,924
The museum notes there was no

140
00:05:00,924 --> 00:05:03,137
fireplace on Viking Age warships,

141
00:05:03,137 --> 00:05:05,816
and later Norwegian law sends the ship's cook

142
00:05:05,816 --> 00:05:08,468
ashore to fetch water and prepare meals.

143
00:05:08,468 --> 00:05:10,279
On a crowded wooden ship,

144
00:05:10,279 --> 00:05:12,523
an open fire is a bold idea.

145
00:05:12,523 --> 00:05:13,945
The same law says the

146
00:05:13,945 --> 00:05:16,335
steersman must not keep the ship at sea

147
00:05:16,335 --> 00:05:18,186
longer than its supplies allow.

148
00:05:18,704 --> 00:05:19,740
A sensible rule.

149
00:05:19,740 --> 00:05:21,384
It is also a reminder that

150
00:05:21,384 --> 00:05:24,399
running out of food was a real possibility,

151
00:05:24,399 --> 00:05:26,079
not a dramatic flourish.

152
00:05:26,549 --> 00:05:28,477
You follow the coast when you can,

153
00:05:28,477 --> 00:05:30,480
cross open water when you must,

154
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:32,927
and bail whenever the sea comes aboard.

155
00:05:32,927 --> 00:05:35,007
The sea comes aboard often.

156
00:05:35,007 --> 00:05:36,659
It does not care how terrifying

157
00:05:36,659 --> 00:05:38,972
your reputation is supposed to be.

158
00:05:38,972 --> 00:05:40,881
Storms were not just uncomfortable.

159
00:05:40,881 --> 00:05:42,672
They killed raiders too.

160
00:05:42,672 --> 00:05:44,214
The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle,

161
00:05:44,214 --> 00:05:46,843
a year-by-year record kept in England,

162
00:05:46,843 --> 00:05:48,504
says that in seven hundred and ninety-four,

163
00:05:48,504 --> 00:05:51,151
raiders who plundered a

164
00:05:51,151 --> 00:05:54,161
Northumbrian monastery lost some of their leaders,

165
00:05:54,161 --> 00:05:56,426
then lost ships to the weather.

166
00:05:56,426 --> 00:05:57,144
Many drowned.

167
00:05:57,144 --> 00:05:58,273
Those who reached the

168
00:05:58,273 --> 00:06:00,324
shore were killed there.

169
00:06:00,324 --> 00:06:01,042
In eight hundred and seventy-seven,

170
00:06:01,042 --> 00:06:05,063
the Chronicle says, the fleet of a Danish

171
00:06:05,063 --> 00:06:07,618
army lost one hundred and twenty ships at Swanage,

172
00:06:07,618 --> 00:06:09,353
on the south coast of England.

173
00:06:09,353 --> 00:06:11,818
Some copies blame a great storm.

174
00:06:11,818 --> 00:06:13,781
Others blame a great mist.

175
00:06:13,781 --> 00:06:16,716
Either way, the sea needed no weapons.

176
00:06:16,716 --> 00:06:19,031
Your own crossing is merely miserable.

177
00:06:19,629 --> 00:06:20,845
You lose a water barrel,

178
00:06:20,845 --> 00:06:23,012
most of the feeling in your toes,

179
00:06:23,012 --> 00:06:25,365
and a significant portion of your dignity.

180
00:06:25,365 --> 00:06:27,669
By the standards of the Chronicle,

181
00:06:27,669 --> 00:06:29,293
that counts as a good trip.

182
00:06:29,827 --> 00:06:30,533
Eventually,

183
00:06:30,533 --> 00:06:33,098
a foreign coast rises out of the grey.

184
00:06:33,659 --> 00:06:35,056
Bjorn has heard about a small

185
00:06:35,056 --> 00:06:37,311
settlement near the shore, with a church.

186
00:06:37,750 --> 00:06:39,789
A church means objects of value,

187
00:06:39,789 --> 00:06:42,627
and people who are not expecting an army.

188
00:06:42,627 --> 00:06:45,581
That is the cold logic of a raid.

189
00:06:45,955 --> 00:06:48,111
Remember who is on the other side.

190
00:06:48,111 --> 00:06:50,642
More than a century and a half

191
00:06:50,642 --> 00:06:52,364
before your voyage,

192
00:06:52,364 --> 00:06:53,338
in seven hundred and ninety-three,

193
00:06:53,967 --> 00:06:56,981
raiders struck the monastery of Lindisfarne.

194
00:06:57,419 --> 00:06:59,368
The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle says

195
00:06:59,368 --> 00:07:02,033
heathen men destroyed God's church there,

196
00:07:02,033 --> 00:07:03,713
with plunder and slaughter.

197
00:07:03,713 --> 00:07:05,755
The scholar Alcuin wrote to the

198
00:07:05,755 --> 00:07:07,829
king of Northumbria that never before

199
00:07:07,829 --> 00:07:10,493
had such terror appeared in Britain.

200
00:07:10,493 --> 00:07:13,150
To you, this is a summer voyage.

201
00:07:13,150 --> 00:07:15,106
To the people on that shore,

202
00:07:15,576 --> 00:07:18,179
it may be the worst day of their lives.

203
00:07:18,179 --> 00:07:21,176
You land in the grey light before morning.

204
00:07:21,176 --> 00:07:23,389
Bjorn's plan sounds simple:

205
00:07:23,827 --> 00:07:26,920
move fast, take what can be carried,

206
00:07:27,278 --> 00:07:29,637
leave before anyone organizes.

207
00:07:29,637 --> 00:07:31,953
Plans always sound simple

208
00:07:31,953 --> 00:07:33,803
before your feet hit cold shingle.

209
00:07:33,803 --> 00:07:36,397
Someone in the settlement sees the sail.

210
00:07:36,397 --> 00:07:38,192
A bell starts ringing.

211
00:07:38,192 --> 00:07:39,428
People run,

212
00:07:39,738 --> 00:07:43,642
carrying children, driving animals, grabbing whatever they can.

213
00:07:43,642 --> 00:07:46,543
Within moments, the place you imagined

214
00:07:46,543 --> 00:07:49,797
full of treasure is mostly full of shouting.

215
00:07:49,797 --> 00:07:51,938
Inside the little church,

216
00:07:51,938 --> 00:07:53,661
the crew finds a silver dish,

217
00:07:53,661 --> 00:07:57,030
some fine cloth and a few decorated fittings.

218
00:07:57,030 --> 00:07:59,031
It is not a mountain of gold.

219
00:07:59,031 --> 00:08:03,305
It is a small community's carefully kept treasure,

220
00:08:03,305 --> 00:08:05,611
and now it is going into a sack.

221
00:08:05,611 --> 00:08:07,621
The crew also takes food:

222
00:08:07,621 --> 00:08:10,879
grain, a few animals, whatever stores are close.

223
00:08:10,879 --> 00:08:13,958
This is the unglamorous side of plunder.

224
00:08:14,299 --> 00:08:15,540
Raiders needed to eat,

225
00:08:15,540 --> 00:08:19,109
and the people they robbed would now face winter without it.

226
00:08:19,109 --> 00:08:20,735
Then comes the part of the

227
00:08:20,735 --> 00:08:23,254
Viking story that the songs skip.

228
00:08:23,254 --> 00:08:25,111
Raiders took people.

229
00:08:25,533 --> 00:08:28,017
Two villagers do not escape in time,

230
00:08:28,017 --> 00:08:31,518
and Bjorn's men force them down to the ship.

231
00:08:31,518 --> 00:08:32,756
This was not rare,

232
00:08:32,756 --> 00:08:35,445
and it was not a side detail.

233
00:08:35,445 --> 00:08:38,158
The Annals of Ulster record that in

234
00:08:38,158 --> 00:08:38,824
eight hundred and twenty-one,

235
00:08:39,144 --> 00:08:41,550
raiders plundered Étar, in Ireland,

236
00:08:41,550 --> 00:08:45,248
and carried off a great number of women into captivity.

237
00:08:45,248 --> 00:08:47,420
The National Museum of Denmark

238
00:08:47,420 --> 00:08:49,209
notes that Vikings acquired slaves

239
00:08:49,209 --> 00:08:51,382
mainly on expeditions to Eastern Europe

240
00:08:51,382 --> 00:08:52,612
and the British Isles,

241
00:08:52,612 --> 00:08:55,531
and sold them at trading centres such as Hedeby.

242
00:08:55,986 --> 00:08:56,966
For the two people now

243
00:08:56,966 --> 00:08:58,039
sitting near the mast,

244
00:08:58,039 --> 00:09:00,165
there is nothing adventurous about this.

245
00:09:00,165 --> 00:09:02,470
Their homes, their families and their

246
00:09:02,470 --> 00:09:05,091
freedom have been taken in a single morning.

247
00:09:05,091 --> 00:09:05,812
From now on,

248
00:09:05,812 --> 00:09:08,109
others will treat them as property.

249
00:09:08,109 --> 00:09:09,308
Eirik is imagined,

250
00:09:09,308 --> 00:09:11,824
but what he is part of here was real,

251
00:09:11,824 --> 00:09:13,621
and it happened on many coasts.

252
00:09:13,980 --> 00:09:16,005
There is no clever joke to make about it,

253
00:09:16,005 --> 00:09:17,365
so we won't make one.

254
00:09:17,836 --> 00:09:19,165
Then the locals come back,

255
00:09:19,165 --> 00:09:21,236
and this time they are not running.

256
00:09:21,594 --> 00:09:23,203
Men arrive with spears,

257
00:09:23,203 --> 00:09:25,873
axes and shields, and more are coming.

258
00:09:25,873 --> 00:09:28,060
Raids worked through surprise.

259
00:09:28,514 --> 00:09:30,206
Once the surprise is gone,

260
00:09:30,206 --> 00:09:31,521
so is the advantage.

261
00:09:31,521 --> 00:09:33,598
Bjorn orders everyone to the ship.

262
00:09:33,598 --> 00:09:35,028
You run with a sack in one

263
00:09:35,028 --> 00:09:37,338
hand and your shield in the other,

264
00:09:37,338 --> 00:09:38,681
which is one hand too few.

265
00:09:38,681 --> 00:09:40,477
You stumble on the shingle,

266
00:09:40,477 --> 00:09:42,186
and the rim of your own shield

267
00:09:42,186 --> 00:09:44,796
opens a long cut across your forearm.

268
00:09:44,796 --> 00:09:45,968
Your first raiding

269
00:09:45,968 --> 00:09:47,510
wound is self-inflicted.

270
00:09:47,510 --> 00:09:49,301
Not everyone is that lucky.

271
00:09:49,301 --> 00:09:52,182
A young oarsman from the next valley,

272
00:09:52,182 --> 00:09:54,989
whose name you only learned at the start of the voyage,

273
00:09:54,989 --> 00:09:57,242
is struck down in the scramble.

274
00:09:57,242 --> 00:09:58,823
The crew gets him aboard.

275
00:09:58,823 --> 00:10:00,909
By evening, he is dead.

276
00:10:00,909 --> 00:10:03,143
There is no ship's doctor.

277
00:10:03,582 --> 00:10:05,530
The Viking Ship Museum notes that

278
00:10:05,530 --> 00:10:07,934
no source or law text mentions actual

279
00:10:07,934 --> 00:10:10,582
medical care on board Viking ships.

280
00:10:10,582 --> 00:10:12,734
Someone binds your arm with a strip

281
00:10:12,734 --> 00:10:15,643
of cloth and tells you not to touch it.

282
00:10:15,643 --> 00:10:17,467
You will touch it constantly.

283
00:10:17,467 --> 00:10:19,945
A cut that needs a few stitches today

284
00:10:19,945 --> 00:10:22,312
could turn dangerous without modern medicine.

285
00:10:22,829 --> 00:10:24,855
For days, you check whether the

286
00:10:24,855 --> 00:10:27,774
redness is spreading, and try to remember

287
00:10:27,774 --> 00:10:29,954
which gods you might have recently offended.

288
00:10:29,954 --> 00:10:31,472
You bury your crewmate on a

289
00:10:31,472 --> 00:10:33,438
quiet stretch of foreign shore.

290
00:10:33,438 --> 00:10:34,627
Back home,

291
00:10:34,627 --> 00:10:37,136
his family might one day raise a stone.

292
00:10:37,136 --> 00:10:38,244
In Småland,

293
00:10:38,244 --> 00:10:41,545
in Sweden, a runestone raised by a man named Vrái for

294
00:10:41,545 --> 00:10:45,474
his brother Gunni ends with one plain sentence:

295
00:10:45,474 --> 00:10:47,613
He died in England.

296
00:10:47,613 --> 00:10:48,424
Sometimes,

297
00:10:48,424 --> 00:10:52,146
raiders did not get away at all.

298
00:10:52,146 --> 00:10:53,678
In two thousand and nine,

299
00:10:53,678 --> 00:10:56,622
archaeologists working ahead of a new road near Weymouth,

300
00:10:56,622 --> 00:10:59,716
in Dorset, found a pit holding around fifty men,

301
00:10:59,716 --> 00:11:02,058
most of them young, all beheaded.

302
00:11:02,402 --> 00:11:03,634
Isotope tests point to

303
00:11:03,634 --> 00:11:06,254
Scandinavian origins for many of them,

304
00:11:06,254 --> 00:11:08,210
and radiocarbon dating places their

305
00:11:08,210 --> 00:11:10,068
deaths around the year one thousand.

306
00:11:10,653 --> 00:11:12,082
Archaeologists note that their

307
00:11:12,082 --> 00:11:14,179
number would fit a single ship's crew.

308
00:11:14,521 --> 00:11:16,831
We do not know exactly who they were,

309
00:11:16,831 --> 00:11:18,802
or why they were killed.

310
00:11:18,802 --> 00:11:20,493
But they are a reminder that the

311
00:11:20,493 --> 00:11:23,076
people on the receiving end of raids could,

312
00:11:23,076 --> 00:11:25,031
and did, strike back.

313
00:11:25,517 --> 00:11:26,248
Back at sea,

314
00:11:26,248 --> 00:11:29,942
the crew finally asks the question everyone has been saving:

315
00:11:29,942 --> 00:11:31,980
what do we actually get?

316
00:11:31,980 --> 00:11:34,363
Here the evidence thins again.

317
00:11:34,363 --> 00:11:35,884
No crew contract from

318
00:11:35,884 --> 00:11:37,686
the Viking Age survives.

319
00:11:37,686 --> 00:11:39,613
Sagas written down centuries

320
00:11:39,613 --> 00:11:42,156
later describe loot being gathered

321
00:11:42,156 --> 00:11:42,993
and shared out,

322
00:11:42,993 --> 00:11:46,520
but they are stories about the past, not pay records.

323
00:11:46,520 --> 00:11:48,089
A reasonable guess,

324
00:11:48,089 --> 00:11:51,133
and it is only a guess, is that the man who

325
00:11:51,133 --> 00:11:52,863
provided the ship and took the

326
00:11:52,863 --> 00:11:55,021
biggest risk expected the biggest share.

327
00:11:55,021 --> 00:11:57,962
In the later Norwegian law the museum describes,

328
00:11:57,962 --> 00:11:58,833
the steersman

329
00:11:58,833 --> 00:12:01,084
owned the ship and held command.

330
00:12:01,084 --> 00:12:02,634
Bjorn does not need a

331
00:12:02,634 --> 00:12:04,520
law to work that out.

332
00:12:04,520 --> 00:12:06,924
So the plunder is laid out on a cloak.

333
00:12:07,394 --> 00:12:08,597
The silver dish.

334
00:12:08,597 --> 00:12:09,589
The fittings.

335
00:12:09,589 --> 00:12:12,630
A bent brooch someone found on the path.

336
00:12:12,630 --> 00:12:15,268
Then somebody picks up an axe,

337
00:12:15,268 --> 00:12:17,356
and the dish stops being a dish.

338
00:12:17,356 --> 00:12:18,973
In the Viking world,

339
00:12:18,973 --> 00:12:22,770
silver was often valued by weight, not by appearance.

340
00:12:23,353 --> 00:12:25,224
Objects were chopped into pieces,

341
00:12:25,224 --> 00:12:28,339
called hacksilver, and weighed on small scales.

342
00:12:28,339 --> 00:12:30,194
Your share of a beautiful dish

343
00:12:30,194 --> 00:12:32,501
may be a jagged triangle of it.

344
00:12:32,501 --> 00:12:34,764
Every piece gets tested.

345
00:12:34,764 --> 00:12:37,187
Archaeologists in Scotland describe

346
00:12:37,187 --> 00:12:40,612
hoards where most of the silver is heavily nicked:

347
00:12:40,612 --> 00:12:42,118
small cuts made as it passed

348
00:12:42,118 --> 00:12:45,027
from hand to hand, to check its purity.

349
00:12:45,027 --> 00:12:46,630
Trust was pleasant.

350
00:12:46,956 --> 00:12:48,011
A knife was better.

351
00:12:48,011 --> 00:12:50,367
You can see this system in the ground.

352
00:12:50,965 --> 00:12:52,669
At Torksey in Lincolnshire,

353
00:12:52,669 --> 00:12:55,900
where a Viking army spent the winter of

354
00:12:55,900 --> 00:12:56,310
eight hundred and seventy-two,

355
00:12:56,310 --> 00:12:59,975
archaeologists from the universities of York

356
00:12:59,975 --> 00:13:02,028
and Sheffield recorded hacksilver,

357
00:13:02,028 --> 00:13:04,300
cut coins, more than a hundred weights,

358
00:13:04,300 --> 00:13:06,723
and hundreds of lead gaming pieces.

359
00:13:07,193 --> 00:13:09,378
Silver, scales and games:

360
00:13:09,378 --> 00:13:11,790
an army weighing its wealth,

361
00:13:11,790 --> 00:13:14,455
then filling long winter evenings.

362
00:13:14,455 --> 00:13:16,157
Nobody recorded who lost

363
00:13:16,157 --> 00:13:17,926
their share on a bad throw.

364
00:13:17,926 --> 00:13:20,055
It is not hard to imagine.

365
00:13:20,055 --> 00:13:21,192
Your own share is a

366
00:13:21,192 --> 00:13:23,027
small pouch of hacksilver.

367
00:13:23,546 --> 00:13:26,303
Enough to pay the smith and fix the roof.

368
00:13:26,303 --> 00:13:28,580
Not enough to change your life.

369
00:13:28,580 --> 00:13:29,628
And there is a darker

370
00:13:29,628 --> 00:13:31,071
part to the arithmetic.

371
00:13:31,734 --> 00:13:32,706
On the way home,

372
00:13:32,706 --> 00:13:35,551
Bjorn sells the two captives at a trading place.

373
00:13:35,551 --> 00:13:36,888
You watch it happen.

374
00:13:36,888 --> 00:13:39,070
Some of the silver in your pouch

375
00:13:39,070 --> 00:13:42,407
exists because two people lost their freedom.

376
00:13:42,407 --> 00:13:44,226
That, too, was Viking wealth.

377
00:13:44,226 --> 00:13:46,544
What about the legendary fortunes?

378
00:13:46,544 --> 00:13:47,623
They existed,

379
00:13:47,623 --> 00:13:50,963
but mostly for fleets led by powerful men.

380
00:13:50,963 --> 00:13:54,365
Decades after Eirik's imagined voyage,

381
00:13:54,365 --> 00:13:55,098
in nine hundred and ninety-one,

382
00:13:55,098 --> 00:13:59,065
the Chronicle records that tribute was paid to the

383
00:13:59,065 --> 00:14:01,832
Danes for the first time: ten thousand pounds.

384
00:14:02,270 --> 00:14:03,996
The payments grew.

385
00:14:03,996 --> 00:14:04,813
In one thousand and twelve,

386
00:14:04,813 --> 00:14:07,995
the Chronicle records forty-eight thousand pounds.

387
00:14:07,995 --> 00:14:10,061
Long before either,

388
00:14:10,061 --> 00:14:14,121
in Frankia, the Annals of Saint-Bertin record that

389
00:14:14,121 --> 00:14:15,271
in eight hundred and forty-five,

390
00:14:15,271 --> 00:14:17,072
King Charles the Bald paid seven

391
00:14:17,072 --> 00:14:20,147
thousand pounds to make a fleet leave Paris.

392
00:14:20,147 --> 00:14:21,166
Huge numbers.

393
00:14:21,166 --> 00:14:23,692
But split across whole armies,

394
00:14:23,692 --> 00:14:25,888
with leaders taking their share first,

395
00:14:25,888 --> 00:14:27,646
the headline shrinks fast.

396
00:14:27,646 --> 00:14:29,574
The men at the oars were not

397
00:14:29,574 --> 00:14:31,835
each carrying home a king's ransom.

398
00:14:31,835 --> 00:14:33,426
One man did want everyone

399
00:14:33,426 --> 00:14:35,232
to know he had done well.

400
00:14:35,232 --> 00:14:36,520
At Orkesta church,

401
00:14:36,520 --> 00:14:40,233
north of Stockholm, a runestone says that a man

402
00:14:40,233 --> 00:14:42,858
named Ulf took three payments in England.

403
00:14:42,858 --> 00:14:45,086
Nobody carves a stone to say:

404
00:14:45,086 --> 00:14:46,823
I went once, cut my arm,

405
00:14:46,823 --> 00:14:49,210
and came back with a small pouch.

406
00:14:49,728 --> 00:14:51,856
Other stones tell the opposite story.

407
00:14:52,695 --> 00:14:54,247
The Swedish National Heritage

408
00:14:54,247 --> 00:14:56,005
Board counts around twenty runestones

409
00:14:56,005 --> 00:14:58,367
in the Mälaren region raised for men

410
00:14:58,367 --> 00:15:01,196
who died on a single expedition to the east,

411
00:15:01,196 --> 00:15:02,847
led by a man named Ingvar.

412
00:15:02,847 --> 00:15:04,009
Their families got

413
00:15:04,009 --> 00:15:05,970
stones instead of homecomings.

414
00:15:05,970 --> 00:15:08,218
Your own voyage home is long,

415
00:15:08,575 --> 00:15:09,847
wet and quiet.

416
00:15:09,847 --> 00:15:11,675
The empty place at the

417
00:15:11,675 --> 00:15:13,262
oar is hard to ignore.

418
00:15:13,262 --> 00:15:16,597
Eventually someone complains about the porridge,

419
00:15:16,597 --> 00:15:18,387
and it feels almost like comfort.

420
00:15:18,387 --> 00:15:20,219
When the Norwegian coast appears,

421
00:15:20,219 --> 00:15:21,686
you realize how little the

422
00:15:21,686 --> 00:15:24,057
farm cares about your adventure.

423
00:15:24,057 --> 00:15:25,695
The fields need harvesting.

424
00:15:25,695 --> 00:15:27,268
The hay is late.

425
00:15:27,268 --> 00:15:28,429
Your neighbor has,

426
00:15:28,429 --> 00:15:31,957
impressively, found something new to argue about.

427
00:15:31,957 --> 00:15:33,383
Aslaug looks at your arm

428
00:15:33,383 --> 00:15:35,700
before she looks at the pouch.

429
00:15:35,700 --> 00:15:37,562
Then she looks at the pouch.

430
00:15:37,562 --> 00:15:39,338
Then back at your arm.

431
00:15:39,809 --> 00:15:42,115
You both understand the exchange rate.

432
00:15:42,115 --> 00:15:43,480
The silver helps.

433
00:15:43,919 --> 00:15:45,145
It pays the smith,

434
00:15:45,145 --> 00:15:48,780
mends the roof, and buys a little security for winter.

435
00:15:48,780 --> 00:15:50,913
It does not make you rich.

436
00:15:51,384 --> 00:15:53,461
It does not make you famous.

437
00:15:53,461 --> 00:15:56,735
It definitely does not make you a legend.

438
00:15:56,735 --> 00:16:00,229
Some raiders never came home at all,

439
00:16:00,229 --> 00:16:02,830
and not only because they died.

440
00:16:02,830 --> 00:16:03,785
In eight hundred and seventy-six,

441
00:16:03,785 --> 00:16:06,532
the Chronicle says, the leader Halfdan shared

442
00:16:06,532 --> 00:16:08,837
out the land of the Northumbrians,

443
00:16:08,837 --> 00:16:11,690
and his men began to plough and support themselves.

444
00:16:12,064 --> 00:16:13,365
So the reward for years

445
00:16:13,365 --> 00:16:15,352
of raiding could be a farm.

446
00:16:15,352 --> 00:16:17,167
The archaeologist Dawn Hadley has

447
00:16:17,167 --> 00:16:19,889
suggested that Halfdan's leading followers

448
00:16:19,889 --> 00:16:21,488
probably took over whole estates,

449
00:16:21,488 --> 00:16:23,736
while the ploughing itself fell to humbler

450
00:16:23,736 --> 00:16:26,665
warriors and the people already living there.

451
00:16:27,038 --> 00:16:27,820
Even in victory,

452
00:16:27,820 --> 00:16:30,191
someone still had to walk behind the plough.

453
00:16:30,725 --> 00:16:33,998
Buried silver tells its own quiet story.

454
00:16:33,998 --> 00:16:35,001
In eighteen forty,

455
00:16:35,373 --> 00:16:39,466
workmen by the River Ribble in Lancashire uncovered the Cuerdale hoard:

456
00:16:39,466 --> 00:16:41,550
about forty kilograms of silver,

457
00:16:41,550 --> 00:16:42,923
including coins,

458
00:16:42,923 --> 00:16:46,217
ingots and hacksilver, hidden in the early nine hundreds.

459
00:16:46,833 --> 00:16:49,677
Whoever buried it never came back for it.

460
00:16:49,677 --> 00:16:51,449
So why was being a Viking

461
00:16:51,449 --> 00:16:53,217
raider worse than you think?

462
00:16:53,217 --> 00:16:56,101
Because the glory belonged to the poems,

463
00:16:56,101 --> 00:16:58,399
and the cold belonged to you.

464
00:16:58,399 --> 00:17:02,217
You rowed, bailed, slept on oars and ate

465
00:17:02,217 --> 00:17:05,433
cold porridge before you ever saw a coast.

466
00:17:05,822 --> 00:17:08,652
You came home with a wound from your own shield,

467
00:17:08,652 --> 00:17:11,316
a crewmate left on a foreign beach,

468
00:17:11,316 --> 00:17:13,135
and a small pouch of silver.

469
00:17:13,135 --> 00:17:15,334
And for the people on the other side

470
00:17:15,334 --> 00:17:17,327
of the raid, it was far worse than that.

471
00:17:17,798 --> 00:17:18,571
Next spring,

472
00:17:18,571 --> 00:17:20,829
Bjorn will talk about another voyage.

473
00:17:20,829 --> 00:17:22,790
You will be fixing the fence.

474
00:17:22,790 --> 00:17:23,249
Again.

475
00:17:23,249 --> 00:17:25,830
And somewhere behind the house,

476
00:17:25,830 --> 00:17:27,563
the sheep has escaped.

477
00:17:27,563 --> 00:17:29,448
This is Viking Chapters:

478
00:17:29,448 --> 00:17:31,565
the lives behind the legends.

479
00:17:31,565 --> 00:17:33,083
If you would like to see what

480
00:17:33,083 --> 00:17:34,026
Eirik does next,

481
00:17:34,026 --> 00:17:37,603
subscribe, and we will meet at the next landing.
